                                            <td class="updated"> 24/Sep/08 06:23 </td>
                                            <td class="resolutiondate"> &nbsp; </td>
                                            <td class="versions">                        1.4.1,                     2.0.0-beta-2            </td>
                                            <td class="fixVersions">    &nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="components">                        Documentation            </td>
                                            <td class="duedate">    &nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="votes">0</td>
                                            <td class="watches">0</td>
                                            <td class="thumbnail"></td>
                                            <td class="timeoriginalestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timeestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timespent"></td>
                                            <td class="workratio">&nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      The name of the attribute *confmappingoverride* is misleading. <br/>
Setting confmappingoverride=true means that extending configurations will *add* to the existing mapping they *don&#39;t override*. <br/>
<br/>
I assume the behaviour is intendet and there is no chance to rename the attribute or change it to actually override. I propose to fix <br/>
the documentation to make this more obvious.<br/>

  </td>
